<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
<title>GitHub Integrations Overview</title>

<style>
body{
    margin:0;
    font-family:Arial, sans-serif;
    background:linear-gradient(135deg,#0d1117,#161b22);
    color:#e6edf3;
    line-height:1.6;
}

header{
    padding:40px 20px;
    text-align:center;
    background:#111827;
    border-bottom:1px solid #30363d;
}

header h1{
    margin:0;
    font-size:28px;
}

.container{
    max-width:900px;
    margin:auto;
    padding:20px;
}

.card{
    background:#161b22;
    border:1px solid #30363d;
    border-radius:12px;
    padding:20px;
    margin-bottom:20px;
    box-shadow:0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
}

h2{
    color:#58a6ff;
    margin-top:0;
}

ul{
    padding-left:20px;
}

code{
    background:#0d1117;
    padding:2px 6px;
    border-radius:6px;
    color:#79c0ff;
}

footer{
    text-align:center;
    padding:20px;
    font-size:12px;
    color:#8b949e;
    border-top:1px solid #30363d;
}
</style>
</head>

<body>

<header>
    <h1>GitHub Integrations</h1>
    <p>Extend GitHub functionality using apps, actions, and APIs</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

    <div class="card">
        <h2>What are Integrations?</h2>
        <p>
            Integrations are tools that extend GitHub’s functionality.
            They can perform actions on GitHub like opening issues, commenting on pull requests,
            and managing projects. They can also react to events happening on GitHub and trigger
            external services like Slack notifications.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h2>Types of Integrations</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>GitHub Apps</b> – Run on an external server or device and interact with GitHub via API.</li>
            <li><b>GitHub Actions</b> – Automate workflows based on GitHub events.</li>
            <li><b>Custom Actions</b> – Reusable code executed inside GitHub Actions workflows.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h2>GitHub APIs</h2>
        <p>
            Integrations use GitHub’s REST and GraphQL APIs to read and modify data.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>REST API – Traditional endpoint-based API</li>
            <li>GraphQL API – Flexible query-based API</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h2>Webhooks</h2>
        <p>
            Webhooks allow integrations to receive real-time updates when events occur on GitHub,
            such as issue creation or pull request updates.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h2>Publishing Integrations</h2>
        <p>
            GitHub Apps and Actions can be published to GitHub Marketplace for public use.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
        <h2>AI in GitHub</h2>
        <p>
            GitHub also supports generative AI integrations, allowing developers to prototype
            and experiment with AI models.
        </p>
    </div>

</div>

<footer>
    Built as a simple HTML documentation page for GitHub Integrations
</footer>

</body>
</html>
